# Quiet-by-Design at MWAA — The CEO's Chair

**Author role:** Chief Executive Officer, large hub airport authority (independent lens)
**Run:** `quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2`
**Date:** 2026-08-07
**Lens:** Long-horizon financial and political positioning — bond covenants, board votes, 30-year amortization, airline consent.

---

## Frame

Every strategist who has been on this board for two years knows that MWAA is a bi-state, federally-leased, 17-member-governed authority carrying the tightest capital cycle in its history. It is also the airport where an Army Black Hawk hit a regional jet in January 2025 and where, eighteen months later, the President and the Secretary of Transportation stood on a stage with the CEO of United Airlines to announce a $20 billion-plus transformation of the international gateway. The question in front of the Council — should MWAA lock in a common quiet-by-design terminal standard now, before the next wave of designs harden — is not, from this chair, a wayfinding or a passenger-experience question. It is a **standards-timing question inside a capital cycle already priced by the rating agencies**, and a **consent question inside a use-and-lease agreement that took effect nineteen months ago and runs until 2039**. That is the currency this brief is written in.

## Key Findings (for a board chair to absorb in one read)

1. **The timing is unusually good and unusually dangerous at the same time.** MWAA plans roughly **$5.5 billion of new debt between 2025 and 2028** and expects net revenue debt service coverage to compress from **1.63× in 2024 toward ~1.3×** over the next five years [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market]. A standards decision that reduces late-stage rework on multi-billion-dollar programs is a straight coverage-defense story to the rating desks. A standards decision that adds recoverable capex or commissioning cost without airline endorsement is exactly the kind of item that shows up in the next Moody's action as "management flexibility narrowing." Same decision, opposite bond narrative. The difference is how it is packaged.

2. **The airline consent window is open for less than it looks.** The 15-year Use and Lease Agreement took effect **January 1, 2025** carrying an approximately **$9 billion capital construction program** at execution [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement]. That program has since expanded past $22 billion at IAD alone [Source: https://www.ffxnow.com/2026/07/31/dulles-partners-mostly-mum-on-details-of-how-22-5b-expansion-plan-will-be-funded/]. Airlines will consent to standards changes that are neutral to their cost per enplanement, or that they can point to as their own idea; they will fight standards that raise their recoverable cost basis without a story they can defend to network planning. The next real airline consent window is not the next agreement — it is inside the current one, wrapped inside the design manual amendments the transformation program is about to demand.

3. **The vehicle already exists — do not invent a new one.** MWAA maintains a seven-volume Design Manual plus Specifications and Estimating and Scheduling Standards, described by the Authority as a mandatory guide with the force of law on airport property, updated annually [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/business/airports-authority-design-manual]. Anything the Council recommends should be framed as an amendment to that manual — a new volume or specification, edited from MASTERSPEC, that binds designers, contractors, and tenants through the mechanism already in use. Standing up a parallel "quiet-airport policy" that lives outside the manual creates two enforcement regimes and the political cost of both. The board will not vote for that twice.

4. **DCA is not a wayfinding problem right now — it is a communications-integrity problem.** The NTSB's February 2026 final report on the January 29, 2025 collision found the probable cause to be the FAA's placement of a helicopter route in close proximity to a runway approach and the FAA's failure to act on prior recommendations [Source: https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/AccidentReports/Reports/AIR2602.pdf]. The political altitude on DCA safety and communications is at Congressional-hearing level. Any standard the Authority adopts that could be characterized — even unfairly — as suppressing safety-relevant announcements will be argued in an oversight hearing before it is argued in a design review. The right posture is to frame quiet-by-design as **intelligibility-by-design**: an ACRP-175-anchored requirement to make safety messages more understandable, not less audible.

5. **The peer evidence is thinner than the McKinsey deck implies.** SFO's frequently-cited "90 minutes of announcements removed, 40% noise reduction" is real reporting but the underlying measurement methodology has not been published in a form a bond investor or a plaintiffs' attorney could stress-test [Source: https://parade.com/travel/this-major-u-s-airport-just-got-a-lot-quieter-thanks-to-a-new-policy]. The international "silent airport" claims (Schiphol, LCY, HEL, CPH, DXB, BCN) have not been surfaced with primary documentation in the context packet. A CEO can cite these as directional; a CEO cannot stake a bond-official-statement paragraph or a use-and-lease amendment on them. The measurable domestic reference is **ACRP Research Report 175, *Improving Intelligibility of Airport Terminal Public Address Systems*** [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/24839/improving-intelligibility-of-airport-terminal-public-address-systems]. That is the anchor.

6. **The board is engineered for a slow-moving standard, not a signature initiative.** MWAA's 17-member board — 7 Virginia, 4 D.C., 3 Maryland, 3 federal, six-year terms — is structurally biased against strategies that require sustained political attention across a chair transition. A quiet-by-design standard framed as a design-manual amendment, sponsored by the Office of Engineering, defended in front of the CFO's coverage forecast, and endorsed by the anchor carriers as neutral-to-favorable to their cost basis, survives any plausible chair change. A standard framed as a CEO signature program does not.

7. **The federal partners live on different calendars, and the standard must survive theirs.** TSA screening throughput, CBP FIS processing, and FAA tower and TRACON coordination each have communication requirements that arrive on their own reauthorization and rulemaking cycles. A common MWAA standard that specifies acoustic and PA-zoning performance in tenant and federal-partner areas — without a defined exception and change-control path for those partners — will collide with the next TSA checkpoint reconfiguration or CBP FIS retrofit and be argued as a barrier to federal operations. The exception path is the standard's insurance policy.

8. **The CEO's job here is not to design the standard — it is to buy the political and financial permission for it.** Board sponsorship, airline consent, federal-partner exception path, rating-agency narrative, and design-manual placement. If those five are secured, the operators and engineers can define STI thresholds, LEQ targets, and PA-zoning geometry inside the existing engineering discipline. If any of those five are missing, no threshold is defensible.

