## Evidence

### Financial and rating context

- MWAA carries ratings of Fitch **AA−**, Moody's **Aa3**, and S&P **AA−**, all stable, most recently affirmed in May 2025 [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market].
- Planned issuance of approximately **$5.5 billion in new debt over the next three years** and projected adjusted debt per O&D enplanement peaking near **$400 in 2028** [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market]. Moody's has flagged that debt per enplanement above $400 could pressure ratings [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market].
- Net revenue debt service coverage projected to fall from **1.63× in 2024** toward approximately **1.3×** across the current five-year window [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market].
- Fitch's Infrastructure & Project Finance airport methodology evaluates airport revenue credits on Revenue Risk (Volume and Price), Infrastructure Development and Renewal, Debt Structure, and Financial Profile — with hub status, O&D enplanement base, and carrier concentration explicitly considered in the Volume assessment [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-bond-ratings].

### Airline use-and-lease context

- The **15-year Use and Lease Agreement took effect January 1, 2025**, carried an approximately $9 billion capital program at execution, and required signature by airlines representing a majority of activity to take effect [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement].
- The IAD transformation announced July 29, 2026 is described as **more than $20 billion**, an increase from the roughly $7 billion previously scoped, and includes replacement of Concourses C and D, extension of the AeroTrain, retirement of the mobile lounges, and preservation of the Saarinen main terminal [Source: https://www.travelpulse.com/news/airlines-airports/washington-dulles-airport-to-undergo-more-than-20-billion-transformation].
- Concourse E is scheduled to open at IAD in fall 2026 with **14 new United gates**, direct AeroTrain access, more than 46,000 sq ft of concessions, and a 40,000-sq-ft airline club [Source: https://www.futuretravelexperience.com/2026/08/mwaa-and-united-airlines-unveil-20-billion-transformation-of-washington-dulles-international-airport/].

### Regulatory and political context

- The **NTSB Final Report AIR-26-02** on the January 29, 2025 midair collision identified the probable cause as the FAA's placement of a helicopter route in close proximity to a runway approach, and the FAA's failure to act on prior collision-risk recommendations near DCA [Source: https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/AccidentReports/Reports/AIR2602.pdf]. Sixty-seven people died.
- Section 502 of the **FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024** (enacted May 16, 2024) directed DOT to grant 10 additional daily slot exemptions for nonstop service — within or beyond the perimeter — at DCA [Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IN12504]. DOT awarded these in December 2024 to Alaska (SAN), American (SAT), Delta (SFO), Southwest, and United (LAS), with service starts staggered from February through March 2025 [Source: https://aviationweek.com/air-transport/airports-networks/airlines-queue-new-dca-slots].

### Standards apparatus and reference research

- MWAA publishes a **seven-volume 2020 Design Manual** plus Specifications and Estimating and Scheduling Standards through the Office of Engineering, with airport-specific supporting volumes and specifications edited from MASTERSPEC [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/business/airports-authority-design-manual].
- **ACRP Research Report 175 — Improving Intelligibility of Airport Terminal Public Address Systems** (Transportation Research Board / FAA-sponsored, 2017, Wilson Ihrig & Associates lead) is the standing U.S. reference document for airport PA intelligibility design guidance [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/24839/improving-intelligibility-of-airport-terminal-public-address-systems].

### Peer practice — reported, not fully verified

- SFO's Quiet Airport policy, launched 2018 and implemented through 2019–2020, is reported to have removed approximately **90 minutes of daily announcement time** and produced a reported **~40% reduction in noise across the terminals** [Source: https://parade.com/travel/this-major-u-s-airport-just-got-a-lot-quieter-thanks-to-a-new-policy]. The measurement methodology has not been surfaced in a primary SFO document within the scope of this brief and should be treated as directional evidence rather than as a threshold to import.

