## The Case *For* Adopting the Standard Now — as the CEO sees it

**1. Standards adopted early are cheaper than change orders adopted late.** IAD Concourse C/D replacement, DCA Terminal 1 replacement, and every follow-on program element in the $20B+ transformation will be scoped, procured, and commissioned inside the next standards window [Source: https://www.travelpulse.com/news/airlines-airports/washington-dulles-airport-to-undergo-more-than-20-billion-transformation]. Every acoustic, PA-zoning, and intelligibility requirement that is not in the design manual before RFP is a change order later — and change orders inside a $22 billion program compound. Standards discipline is a coverage-defense strategy.

**2. The rating agencies read management flexibility as a credit strength.** Moody's has explicitly cited management's "proven track record of prudently executing the capital plan and maintaining a healthy capital structure and debt service coverage" [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market]. A design-manual amendment that codifies commissioning and post-occupancy verification requirements — visible, auditable, and applied across two airports — is exactly the kind of governance signal that appears in the "management" section of a rating report.

**3. It reframes a post-collision safety story.** DCA's public narrative right now is a communications-integrity narrative. A standard that raises measurable speech transmission index thresholds and mandates redundant visual and digital communication paths — anchored to ACRP-175 — is a credible answer to the "what has the airport done" question that follows every NTSB final report. It is defensible in a Senate Commerce hearing in a way that a passenger-experience initiative is not.

**4. It gives the airlines a story to sign.** A common two-airport standard that reduces commissioning risk on IAD Concourse E, Concourse C/D replacement, and DCA Terminal 1 replacement — without materially raising airline cost per enplanement — is exactly the kind of item the anchor carriers will endorse if given a seat at the drafting table. United, given its 14 new IAD gates and its co-branded transformation announcement [Source: https://www.futuretravelexperience.com/2026/08/mwaa-and-united-airlines-unveil-20-billion-transformation-of-washington-dulles-international-airport/], has incentives aligned with a strong IAD passenger experience. American at DCA has incentives aligned with anything that raises the ceiling on the post-collision passenger experience.

**5. Federal political altitude is momentarily favorable.** The July 2026 IAD announcement was a coordinated federal-airport rollout with the President and the Transportation Secretary on stage [Source: https://www.transportation.gov/briefing-room/prepare-takeoff-president-donald-j-trump-us-transportation-secretary-sean-p-duffy]. Standards that visibly upgrade the international-gateway passenger experience land inside that political narrative rather than against it.

## The Case *Against* — the same CEO, honestly

**1. The coverage window is exactly the wrong window for optional cost.** MWAA is heading into its tightest coverage compression in a decade. Every recoverable dollar the standard adds — commissioning scope, post-occupancy verification, tenant enforcement — flows into rates and charges and shows up in the airlines' cost per enplanement. In a 1.3× coverage environment, "optional" is a fighting word.

**2. Airline consent is not free.** The Use and Lease took effect nineteen months ago. Reopening the operational or cost-recovery envelope inside it for anything other than a signature safety or transformation item burns political capital that the CEO will need again the next time the transformation program requires an airline vote.

**3. Standards written in haste bind for thirty years.** A specification amendment issued before ACRP-175 is properly translated into MWAA-specific STI, LEQ, and PA-zoning thresholds — and before the exception path for TSA, CBP, and FAA partner spaces is drafted — is a specification the Office of Engineering will spend the next decade waiving. Waivers are how design manuals lose the "force of law" description MWAA uses about them today.

**4. The peer evidence is thin.** The international quiet-airport case rests on secondary reporting that has not been surfaced in primary form. Betting a rating-narrative paragraph on SFO's 40% number without a verified methodology is exactly the kind of claim that comes back in a Fitch review as "management overstated peer benchmarking."

**5. Signature initiatives do not survive chair transitions well.** MWAA's board turnover is engineered for continuity of institution, not continuity of any given CEO's initiative. A standard packaged as a governance amendment survives. A standard packaged as this-CEO's-initiative does not.

## Data Points and Precedents a Strategist Can Use

1. **Coverage arithmetic.** Coverage moving from 1.63× (2024) toward ~1.3× (five-year forward) while $5.5B of new debt is issued 2025–2028 [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market]. Any standard added to recoverable capex should be modeled against a 1.3× baseline, not the 2024 headroom.
2. **Debt per enplanement ceiling.** Adjusted debt per O&D enplanement projected to peak near $400 in 2028, with $400 identified as the pressure threshold in the Moody's read [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-goes-to-market]. That is the number in every rating-agency briefing for the next three years.
3. **Use-and-lease vintage and scope.** 15-year term effective January 1, 2025, approximately $9B capital program at execution, now expanded past $22B at IAD alone [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement]; [Source: https://www.ffxnow.com/2026/07/31/dulles-partners-mostly-mum-on-details-of-how-22-5b-expansion-plan-will-be-funded/]. Any amendment lives inside this envelope for the next 13 years.
4. **NTSB probable cause language.** "The probable cause of this accident was the Federal Aviation Administration's (FAA) placement of a helicopter route in close proximity to a runway approach; their failure to regularly review and evaluate helicopter routes and available data, and their failure to act on recommendations to mitigate the risk of a midair collision near Ronald Reagan Washington National Airport" [Source: https://www.ntsb.gov/investigations/AccidentReports/Reports/AIR2602.pdf]. This is the sentence a strategist should quote back to anyone who argues quiet-by-design is a comfort initiative.
5. **The anchor standard document.** ACRP Research Report 175, published 2017 by the Transportation Research Board, is the domestic reference every MWAA specification should cite [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/24839/improving-intelligibility-of-airport-terminal-public-address-systems]. It is the citation that turns "quiet airport" from a branding phrase into an engineering specification.

---

## Recommendation from the Chair

Frame quiet-by-design as **intelligibility-by-design**, anchored to ACRP-175, and vehicle it as a Design Manual amendment sponsored by the Office of Engineering with visible airline drafting participation. Position it to the rating desks as a commissioning-discipline and rework-avoidance measure — a coverage-defense story, not a passenger-experience story. Reserve any airline-cost-impact provisions for a defined exception path with change control. Do not put it on the CEO's signature-initiative list; put it on the CFO's coverage-forecast defense and the Chief Engineer's design-manual annual update. That is how it survives a chair transition, a rating review, and the next NTSB hearing.
